From 966b099dbeea6128c9c9c25254a08506cc86b9b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: silo-research <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 14:03:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] gvproxy: add -dns-upstream

The embedded DNS server resolves the names outside its zones with the
host's resolver. That is right for most users, but not for two cases:

- A guest that must not reach the outside world at all can still send
  data out in names: a lookup of <data>.attacker.example travels from
  the host's resolver to the attacker's name server, whatever the guest
  may connect to.
- An embedder that decides which destinations the guest may reach wants
  the guest's lookups answered by its own resolver, which can refuse a
  name or log it, rather than by the host's.

Configuration gains DNSUpstream, and gvproxy -dns-upstream (repeatable)
and `stack.dnsUpstream` in the configuration file. Each entry is a
server's "ip:port", which then answers every record type for the names
outside the zones, through the same code paths as the host's resolver,
so answers have the same shape.
The single entry "none" answers REFUSED and asks nobody. When it is not
set, nothing changes.
